Your task is to perform necessary operations and measurements to figure out which state it was and to return 0 if it was a ![image](https://espresso.codeforces.com/c1f9019c20fc8a9610d31c36ba7860cef0b7c55c.png) state or 1 if it was ![image](https://espresso.codeforces.com/891b1e75c93e44f237b7fde497062f8e85c1d952.png) state. The state of the qubit after the operations does not matter.

Note that these states are not orthogonal, and thus can not be distinguished perfectly. In each test your solution will be called 1000 times, and your goal is to get a correct answer at least 80% of the times. In each test ![image](https://espresso.codeforces.com/c1f9019c20fc8a9610d31c36ba7860cef0b7c55c.png) and ![image](https://espresso.codeforces.com/891b1e75c93e44f237b7fde497062f8e85c1d952.png) states will be provided with 50% probability.

You have to implement an operation which takes a qubit as an input and returns an integer.

Your code should have the following signature:

```
namespace Solution {
    open Microsoft.Quantum.Primitive;
    open Microsoft.Quantum.Canon;

    operation Solve (q : Qubit) : Int
    {
        body
        {
            // your code here
        }
    }
}
```

